task: adversarial-lanes
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-06
branch: feature/adversarial-lanes
worktree: ../okay-wt-adv

why: every table in docs/benchmarks.md is a shape this project chose,
  and after a day of corrections that all went our way the credible
  move is to add the rows likely to go against us. Those are the
  shapes the cats/ZIO runtimes are built for and their per-bind tax
  buys: many fibers under real contention, cancellation, fork/join
  as THROUGHPUT rather than fixed cost.

what: AdversarialBenchmark, okay / ZIO / cats-effect (kyo where the
  spelling is obvious), each lane the competitor's own idiom checked
  in its source per AGENTS.md:
  1. fork/join throughput: 10 000 fibers each doing a small step,
     all joined (§4 is K=100, a fixed-cost measure)
  2. many-to-many contention: P producers x C consumers over one
     bounded queue, frequent park/unpark (ManyProducers is P x 1)
  3. cancellation: K fibers parked on an await, all cancelled, all
     joined -- interruption cost per fiber

prediction, before the numbers: (1) ZIO/cats within 1-2x of okay or
  ahead -- a work-stealing pool amortises what a virtual thread pays
  per start; (2) ZIO ahead at C>1 -- its queue's consumer side is
  cheaper than a channel transaction; (3) cats/ZIO ahead -- their
  cancellation is a fiber flag, ours is a thread interrupt.
  Disqualifier for the exercise itself: if okay wins all three, the
  lanes were chosen badly and it says so, not that we are unbeatable.
